# TODO - Reference behaviour fixtures

## Objective

Establish reproducible behaviour comparisons from lawful local references.

## Acceptance

- Use appropriate reference execution tools and synthetic public fixtures.
- Record unsupported or unverified behaviour without claiming parity.
- Do not distribute proprietary inputs, executable code or assets.

## Evidence

The verified Simpsons World four-player set 1 reference archive has 14
members matching the pinned MAME driver definition at revision
`cdcbdf509292257dcdaedc8d8452f79d89ff20d4`. Driver metadata specifies four
independent player and coin inputs, a 320 by 224 visible raster, and a
16,896,000 ns frame period (384 by 264 clocks at 6 MHz). The local source
metadata also establishes two-lane tile and four-lane sprite word layouts.

The shared importer implements explicit word interleaving and bit-offset
indexed tile decoding; synthetic fixtures validate lane order, plane
significance, row and column order, and rejection without partial output.
The selected source adapter adds synthetic fixtures for the exact tile and
sprite layouts, including the sprite nibble permutation.

The initial full MAME source download timed out.
No remake parity is claimed. Reference files remain
private under `.reference/`.

The shared fixed-tick replay now compares ordered public observations after
each checkpoint tick, retaining the first mismatch and rejecting skipped,
duplicate or unfinished checkpoints. Synthetic tests exercise different
render frame partitions. A local MAME reference run is now available; see
the Simpsons task for repeatable original observations. Those observations
are not yet compared against remake gameplay.

Live simulations now own the same fixed clock and public InputStep
contract as scripted replays. Explicit limits bound pending action count
and future tick distance. Admission returns the entire rejected batch
on capacity, late or unreachable/future tick errors, and accepted actions
are consumed only at their assigned ticks in insertion order. Synthetic
tests compare the exact live and replayed public action sequences across
different frame partitions, including idle and backlog-draining ticks.
They also verify atomic rejection and clock-overflow recovery.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795584092916684839-3725186.
A title host must still map native event timing to assigned ticks; no
physical input latency or original-game equivalence is implied.

Native Linux input now exposes elapsed SDL milliseconds and reconstructs
32-bit event timestamps across a counter wrap against the 64-bit clock.
Events from before the input session start map to zero; an ambiguous or
future timestamp is rejected under the documented polling interval.
The shared live queue maps absolute elapsed timestamps to fixed tick
indices, including exact boundaries, independently of render partitions.
It preserves the rejected batch if a timestamp is late or unrepresentable.

The native accelerated smoke routes a queued SDL keyboard event through
the logical action mapping and timestamped queue into a synthetic fixed
update. Synthetic tests cover timestamp wrap, pre-start input, boundaries,
and equivalent action delivery under different frame partitions. No
physical input latency, gameplay parity or actual device timing is
established; the SDL timestamps have millisecond granularity. A title
host must decide explicitly how to handle delayed or rejected releases.

The shared audio frame clock maps explicitly supplied elapsed simulation
intervals to complete PCM output frames with exact fractional carry. At
48 kHz, 125 intervals of 16,896 microseconds yield 101,376 frames: 124
intervals of 811 frames and one of 812. Tests compare that partition with
one combined duration, verify zero-duration behavior, and check that a
rejected overflow does not discard fractional time. No native device
clock, output latency, original event timing or audio parity is implied.
